course: dictionary
lesson: 9267
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words around it, tells you which meaning is right. «The sovereign of the country gave a speech to the people.» Here sovereign means a king or queen, the ruler of a land.
explain: Now read this: «The aunt handed over certain gold sovereigns, each worth one pound.» Sovereign here is a shiny old coin, not a person. The clues are gold and worth one pound.
explain: Sovereign can also be a describing word. In «a sovereign country», it means a country with the full right to rule itself, without control from other countries. Ask yourself: is the word naming a person or thing, or describing one? That question helps you choose the right meaning.
fact: A sovereign is a king or queen, the ruler of a country.
fact: A sovereign is also an old British gold coin worth one pound.
fact: As an adjective, sovereign means having the full right to rule itself, as in «a sovereign country».
fact: Sovereign can also mean very great or best, as in «a sovereign remedy» (an old, formal use).
exercise: «The sovereign of the country gave a speech.» What does sovereign mean here? | *A king or queen, the ruler of a country | An old gold coin | A type of bonnet | A kind of shawl
exercise: «The aunt handed over certain gold sovereigns.» What is a sovereign in this sentence? | A ruler of a country | *An old gold coin worth one pound | A rusty black bonnet | A cotton print gown
exercise: What does sovereign mean in «a sovereign country»? | Made of gold | *Having the full right to rule itself, without control from other countries | Hungry and tired | Very tall and very old
test: In «The sovereign gave a speech to the nation», what does sovereign mean? | A gold coin worth one pound | A cotton print gown | *A king or queen | A rusty black bonnet
test: In «a sovereign remedy», sovereign means | An old gold coin | *Very great or best | A ruler of a country | Full of rust
exam: Why does sovereign mean a coin, not a ruler, in «The aunt handed over certain gold sovereigns»? | Because the word has no meaning at all | Because aunts are always rulers | Because the sentence is about a castle | *Because the words gold and worth one pound show it is money
